Restrictions on pet stores concerning dogs:
(a) No owner, manager, or employee of a pet store shall negligently display, offer for sale, deliver, barter, auction, broker, give away, transfer, or sell any live dog from a pet store to a person unless the dog was obtained from one of the following sources:
(1) A qualified breeder;
(2) Dog broker that is licensed by the United States Department of Agriculture under 7 U.S.C. § 2133 and, if applicable, a state agency, and who obtains their dogs from:
a. A qualified breeder; or
b. A hobby breeder that is vetted by the dog broker to ensure that the hobby/breeder is in compliance with all applicable federal or state rules and regulations that may be applicable to such hobby breeder;
(3) An animal rescue for dogs;
(4) An animal shelter for dogs; or
(5) A humane society.
(b) No owner, manager, or employee of a pet store shall negligently sell, deliver, barter, auction, broker, give away, or transfer any of the following:
(1) A dog that is less than eight weeks old;
(2) A dog without a health certificate signed by a licensed veterinarian;
(3) A dog that does not have a permanent implanted identification microchip;
(4) A dog to a person who is younger than 18 years of age as verified by valid photo identification;
(5) A dog acquired from a qualified breeder or dog broker unless the owner, manager, or employee provides to the person acquiring the dog, at a time prior to the transaction for the acquisition of the dog by the pet store, a written certification and documentation that includes all of the following information:
a. The name of the breeder that bred the dog;
b. The address, if available, of the breeder that bred the dog;
c. The United States Department of Agriculture license number of the breeder that bred the dog, if applicable, and a copy of the most current United States Department of Agriculture inspection report for the breeder;
d. The dog's birth date, if known;
e. The date that the pet store took possession of the dog;
f. The breed, gender, color, and any identifying marks of the dog, if any;
g. A certificate of veterinarian inspection, or other similar document, signed by a licensed veterinarian that describes any known disease, illness, or congenital or hereditary condition that adversely affects the health of the dog at the time of examination;
(6) A dog acquired from a qualified breeder or dog broker unless all of the following information regarding the dog is available to the general public at the pet store:
a. The name of the breeder that bred the dog;
b. The address, if available, of the breeder that bred the dog;
c. The United States Department of Agriculture license number of the breeder that bred the dog;
d. The dog's birth date, if known;
e. The breed of the dog.
(c) No owner, manager, or employee of a pet store shall knowingly or recklessly alter or provide false information on a certification or information provided in accordance with Subsection (b)(5) of this section.
(d) Each dog obtained by a pet store for resale shall be placed in a holding unit for a minimum of four days after receipt by the pet store and until such time period has expired, the pet store shall not allow such animal to be comingled within the general population of other animals held for resale by the pet store.
(e) If, at any time, any dog shows any sign of a communicable illness, disease or ailment, such dog shall be moved to an isolation unit until the dog does not show any signs of any communicable illness, disease or ailment.
(f) Each pet store shall permit a person purchasing a dog the right to return the dog to the pet store for any reason within 48 hours of purchase, and shall offer such person the option to either:
(1) Exchange such dog for another dog of equal or lesser value of the dog originally purchased; or
(2) Cancel the sale and return the dog to the pet store and receive the original amount paid for such dog less a reasonable restocking fee not to exceed 50 percent of the original purchase price for the dog.
(g) Each pet store must offer warranties on each dog purchased that are no less favorable then the following:
(1) A health warranty for the first ten days after the date of consumer possession; provided the pet store may require certification of any ailment by a licensed veterinarian and place such other reasonable requirements to activate such warranty. If a licensed veterinarian provides a written diagnosis that the dog has a serious physical health problem that makes the dog a poor health risk, and the person otherwise complies with the terms of the warranty, the pet store shall offer such person the option to either:
a. Exchange such dog for another dog of equal or lesser value of the dog originally purchased;
b. Obtain a store credit for the original purchase price of the dog; or
c. Cancel the sale and return the dog to the pet store and receive the original amount paid for such dog with no restocking fee.
(2) A hereditary or congenital disorder warranty for at least the first year after the date of delivery; provided the pet store may require certification of any hereditary or congenital disorder by a licensed veterinarian and place such other reasonable requirements to activate such warranty, and if the dog is diagnosed with a hereditary or congenital disorder, and the person otherwise complies with the terms of the warranty, the pet store shall offer such person a store credit of not less than 50 percent of the original purchase price of the dog.
(3) Nothing set forth in this Subsection (g) shall be deemed to:
a. Prohibit the pet store from offering additional warranties or health plans covering any dog purchased from the pet store; or
b. Prevent the sale of a dog by the pet store without any warranties if the dog was sold at a negotiated price discounted from the standard or current sales price for the dog.
(h) Each pet store must provide and/or administer appropriate ongoing vaccinations and preventative care for all dogs held in inventory for resale as determined by the pet store's or attending licensed veterinarian.
(i) Each pet store shall provide timely, reasonable and adequate care and medication for any dog in its custody and control that is exhibiting any signs of illness or a health related injury or ailment.
(Ord. No. 464-2023, 10-9-2023)

